Question 8: Collision Detection: A circular Enemy Hitbox is centred at (300, 250) with a radius of 20 pixels. The player’s position is (310, 265). Is the player within the enemy’s hit box range? 

The hitbox is centred at (300, 250).
Radius = 20 pixels
The player's Position is (310, 265).

First I will calculate the distance between the player and the centre of the circle. 
Distance = √ (X2  - X1)² + (Y² - Y1)²
Distance = √ (310 - 300)² + (265 - 250)²
Distance = √ (10)² + (15)²
Distance = √ 100 + 225 = √ 325
Distance = √325: finding a perfect square 

= √325 / 25 = 13
= √325 = √25 × 13 = 5 × √13

Turning 13 into a decimal is √13 because 4 multiplied by 4 will be 16 and 3 multiplied by 3 will be 9. The answer lies between 3 and 4, = 3.6055.

Then I bring down 5 × √13, replacing 13 with 3.6055.

5 multiplied by 3.6055 = 18.075 – rounding off to 18.03

D = 18.03

When 18.03 is compared to the given radius of 20, it is lower, which means the distance is less than the radius and the player is within the enemy’s hitbox range.


